Hey Bruno, thanks so much for joiningining us on the podcast today.

10
00:01:14,560 --> 00:01:14,880
Hey, David.

11
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:16,000
Thanks for having me.

12
00:01:16,160 --> 00:01:16,560
Hi, Jacob.

13
00:01:16,800 --> 00:01:18,320
Really nice chatting with you today as well.

14
00:01:18,560 --> 00:01:19,600
Always, David, always.

15
00:01:19,920 --> 00:01:27,040
But I'm very excited to talk to Bruno because you were one of, I think, the fifteen people who signed up for RevenueCat in 2017.

16
00:01:27,600 --> 00:01:35,200
Like, well, we launched in December, you poked around, we never made it happen for a while, but like you were very, very, very, very, very early.

17
00:01:35,520 --> 00:01:38,080
And somehow we've never talked directly before.

18
00:01:38,480 --> 00:01:47,680
But I always joke that the, I think about probably a lot of people like this, but I I always have the first twenty sign-ups to RevenueCat burned in my head because I looked at them in the database so many times.

19
00:01:47,840 --> 00:01:50,640
So I'm very excited to get the backstory today.

20
00:01:50,960 --> 00:01:53,840
So yeah, you get some convincing, but you find that he gets me.

21
00:01:54,400 --> 00:01:57,200
Yeah, you know, five years later, or no, more than that, seven years later.

22
00:01:58,640 --> 00:02:09,200
Well, speaking of a long time in the making, Genius Scan and your company has been a many years journey, almost fifteen years in the making.

23
00:02:09,440 --> 00:02:22,560
So I did want to kind of go back to the founding, because I think a lot of people will be interested to hear that whole story of how you got started, how you got that early product market fit, and then how you grew that to what today is a really incredible business.

24
00:02:22,800 --> 00:02:29,680
You have five million MAU, ten percent team like that's it's a fantastic success story for a bootstrapped app.

25
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:31,280
But yeah, tell us the story.

26
00:02:31,520 --> 00:02:41,120
Yeah, so Genius Can is the main app that we develop as a mobile app development studio and we started it like fifteen years, almost fifteen years ago.

27
00:02:41,280 --> 00:02:42,480
It will be fifteen years in June.

28
00:02:42,960 --> 00:02:45,760
Actually, we didn't want to, I mean, we didn't plan to start Genius Can.

29
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:48,240
So it was my co-founder and myself.

30
00:02:48,400 --> 00:02:52,000
We were students at the University of Illinois and we were roommates.

31
00:02:52,400 --> 00:02:54,240
iPhone was quite new at that time.

32
00:02:54,560 --> 00:02:56,960
I mean, well, I guess iPhone was 2007.

33
00:02:57,360 --> 00:03:00,880
Yeah, well, I think it's 2007, the iPhone, but then the app store just opened.

34
00:03:01,600 --> 00:03:05,120
publicly to third party apps in 2009, right?

35
00:03:05,360 --> 00:03:07,360
I think at the 2008.

36
00:03:07,760 --> 00:03:08,640
2008, okay.

37
00:03:09,120 --> 00:03:13,600
Yeah, so in 2009, basically, I mean, I was like, oh, maybe you should make an app, it would be fun.

38
00:03:13,840 --> 00:03:22,240
I was more like in Linux development before, but my co-founder was in, my roommate, I guess, at the time was in doing image processing research.

39
00:03:22,800 --> 00:03:24,320
And so we thought, what could we do with that?

40
00:03:24,640 --> 00:03:30,240
We brainstormed and we had this great idea, it was to make an app to scan paintings in museums.

41
00:03:31,040 --> 00:03:40,800
You would go to the Louvre in France, you would sc get all the information about it, like who painted it.

42
00:03:41,120 --> 00:03:43,600
Okay, so it was like augmented, almost augmented reality.

43
00:03:44,320 --> 00:03:48,640
Yeah, it was augmented reality, like, you know, years before it was something.

44
00:03:50,320 --> 00:03:51,520
So we started working on that.

45
00:03:52,080 --> 00:03:56,560
After a while, I mean, we had it kind of working, but then we had two problems.

46
00:03:56,880 --> 00:03:59,840
One was that we used a technology which was called SIFT.

47
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:08,000
It was like, basically, features that you embed in an image and key points that you can identify to recognize an image under any angle.

48
00:04:08,720 --> 00:04:12,960
I mean, yeah, it was a problem with this technology.

49
00:04:13,360 --> 00:04:15,040
is that there were some patents on it.

50
00:04:15,520 --> 00:04:22,880
I knew at the time we were just students in a dorm room, I guess, and we were like patents we were going to be sued by Americans, you know what?

51
00:04:23,600 --> 00:04:26,800
This was in late 2000, so like 2008 and 2009.

52
00:04:26,960 --> 00:04:30,960
So this was before like the modern AI revolution and all this stuff.

53
00:04:31,200 --> 00:04:33,920
So this would have been like they wouldn't even call it machine learning at the time.

54
00:04:34,080 --> 00:04:35,760
They would have called it like vision.

55
00:04:36,400 --> 00:04:38,000
Yeah, it was computer vision.

56
00:04:38,160 --> 00:04:38,640
Basically, yeah.

57
00:04:38,720 --> 00:04:42,480
And actually, my room cousin, Guillaume, was doing computer vision at the university.

58
00:04:43,440 --> 00:04:46,000
The kids don't have any idea how good they have it these days.

59
00:04:46,480 --> 00:04:50,880
So they'll slap image recognition in like with one API call.

60
00:04:51,520 --> 00:04:54,640
Throw more transformers at it, it'll figure it out eventually, you know?

61
00:04:54,960 --> 00:04:57,200
But yeah, CV was like a different ball game.

62
00:04:57,600 --> 00:05:02,960
So the patent scared you and so yeah, I mean, we were, I mean, there were alternatives.

63
00:05:03,280 --> 00:05:05,440
There were like, you know, open source alternatives.

64
00:05:05,680 --> 00:05:15,360
And then also there was another problem that at that time, like, if you imagine someone going to the Louvre in Paris to scan pictures, it probably was a tourist, you know, coming from China or the US.

65
00:05:15,920 --> 00:05:17,920
And at that time there was no, like, cellular.

66
00:05:18,160 --> 00:05:20,960
I mean, when you went abroad, like roaming fees were crazy high.

67
00:05:21,200 --> 00:05:24,800
So when I went to Europe in 2010, I had no cell phone for three weeks, right?

68
00:05:25,200 --> 00:05:26,560
Like I went to internet cafes.

69
00:05:27,360 --> 00:05:27,760
Exactly.

70
00:05:28,560 --> 00:05:31,440
So you would have to download the database in advance on your phone.

71
00:05:31,600 --> 00:05:32,400
It was like huge, right?

72
00:05:32,560 --> 00:05:37,120
Because the database of images to, I mean, even because we only had signatures of the images.

73
00:05:37,360 --> 00:05:38,240
So it would be huge.

74
00:05:38,400 --> 00:05:42,000
It would take, I mean, and so we were sort of like, I mean, who's going to do that?

75
00:05:42,800 --> 00:05:51,120
But the thing is that when preparing this app, so the way Guillaume was doing, he was like taking photos of Van Gogh painting on his desk all the time.

76
00:05:51,840 --> 00:05:58,800
And actually, we, I mean, we were detecting the painting on his desk and we were like, okay, we could detect any kind of document like that.

77
00:05:59,040 --> 00:06:00,720
And that's how we, we thought, okay, maybe.

78
00:06:01,280 --> 00:06:05,920
we will start doing a document scanning app and we'll go back to the paintings later.

79
00:06:06,880 --> 00:06:11,200
And so, you know, fifteen years later, we are still on the document scanning.

80
00:06:11,680 --> 00:06:12,880
And that's how it started.

81
00:06:13,120 --> 00:06:20,480
And the funny story is that I was, I mean, what triggered actually the start of the development was in, I think it's may 2009.

82
00:06:20,720 --> 00:06:24,880
So we had basically the tech, but it was in developed in MATLAB on the computer.

83
00:06:25,040 --> 00:06:26,160
It was not like a mobile app.

84
00:06:26,880 --> 00:06:31,440
And in may 2009, I was going back to France for a wedding.

85
00:06:31,760 --> 00:06:37,440
And actually, I got stuck in the Chicago O'Hare Airport because of the Icelandic volcano.

86
00:06:37,760 --> 00:06:39,760
You remember, like, I don't remember the name.

87
00:06:39,920 --> 00:06:42,000
It's like I used the usual rule.

88
00:06:42,400 --> 00:06:49,280
And so I was actually I had flown to Chicago and I got my ticket refunded by the airline.

89
00:06:49,600 --> 00:06:51,600
I don't know how, but it happened.

90
00:06:51,920 --> 00:06:54,960
And I was like, I saved the money of the trip to Paris.

91
00:06:55,760 --> 00:07:03,200
And so I went to the Apple store in Chicago, purchased a MacBook because I didn't have one at the time, and went back to Champagne, Illinois.

92
00:07:03,360 --> 00:07:05,600
And that's where I started, sorry, I started developing the app.

93
00:07:06,080 --> 00:07:15,920
And actually, what really strikes me is that that was in May and in June 22 June 2010, the app was launched in the App Store.

94
00:07:16,320 --> 00:07:21,760
So in a basically the first version of the app was a month of development, a month and a half maybe of development.

95
00:07:22,080 --> 00:07:23,520
And it picked up really quickly.

96
00:07:23,680 --> 00:07:31,200
So I don't have the exact numbers in mind, but it was like we put it as a free app in the App Store and it just set up the rankings.

97
00:07:31,680 --> 00:07:34,560
Was it the first or one of the early scanning apps?

98
00:07:34,800 --> 00:07:36,720
I mean, there was another one.

99
00:07:37,520 --> 00:07:38,640
Yeah, I think it was the first app.

100
00:07:39,120 --> 00:07:42,800
Well, I mean, like the first that was working quite well.

101
00:07:43,280 --> 00:07:54,880
Yeah, this was like one of the non-obvious, like life changing, I don't know, important utilities that a great camera and a good phone could do because I never it never crossed my mind that, like, oh, I don't need a scanner anymore.

102
00:07:55,040 --> 00:08:00,640
Like, I can I can get a good enough scan just with an app that knows what it's doing in a camera.

103
00:08:01,280 --> 00:08:05,840
And so, yeah, I can imagine being one of the first and like just locking that down.

104
00:08:06,640 --> 00:08:16,080
Yeah, and it was kind of magic because for users, I think that was the way it worked really well because it was like one of the first apps that kind of bridged the physical world and the digital world.

105
00:08:16,320 --> 00:08:18,640
It's like it's almost like an AR, right?

106
00:08:18,960 --> 00:08:23,040
You take a document and then you we would warp it and present it to you very clean.

107
00:08:23,280 --> 00:08:25,600
So it was I think it was fun to use.

108
00:08:26,240 --> 00:08:31,920
And the plan was to put it as a free app for a few days because that was what everyone, you know, this was like a launch offer.

109
00:08:32,240 --> 00:08:32,800
So we would do that.

110
00:08:33,040 --> 00:08:36,400
And then after a few days, we would just put it paid and then, you know, make some money.

111
00:08:36,800 --> 00:08:38,960
And so after a few days, I put it as a paid app.

112
00:08:39,520 --> 00:08:44,960
And immediately we went from like, I don't know, thousands of downloads a day to one, two downloads a day.

113
00:08:45,360 --> 00:08:47,600
I think it was 99 cents to be, to be.

114
00:08:48,400 --> 00:08:48,480
Wow.

115
00:08:49,040 --> 00:08:49,120
Wow.

116
00:08:49,360 --> 00:08:51,040
That just shows, and this is 2010.

117
00:08:51,120 --> 00:08:57,680
So this is like, I think people forget how much debasement there was of apps in the early days of the App Store.

118
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:08,480
Like there was a free fall from like being able to make money like selling an app for, you know, ten dollars or twenty dollars on the desktop to a race to the bottom happened very, very quickly.

119
00:09:09,040 --> 00:09:10,640
Yeah, that was a race to the bottom.

120
00:09:11,840 --> 00:09:14,880
So basically we panicked and we put it back as a free app.

121
00:09:15,200 --> 00:09:16,640
We were like, we worry later.

122
00:09:16,800 --> 00:09:19,440
I mean, we were just students, you know, we didn't have to make a living from that.

123
00:09:19,600 --> 00:09:22,560
Yeah, I mean, a few thousand free users is better than two dollars a day.

124
00:09:22,960 --> 00:09:23,520
You know what I mean?

125
00:09:23,840 --> 00:09:28,720
Like after that, we were lucky to be featured by Apple in the months after that.

126
00:09:29,120 --> 00:09:40,480
I think that was a summer, I may remember wrong, but where iAds launched, so the supposedly super high quality ads from Apple and they had like at the time they had like crazy high eCPMs.

127
00:09:40,640 --> 00:09:45,280
I think you had like $10 eCPMs or I know like $30 and $40.

128
00:09:45,760 --> 00:09:46,960
I launched with iAd.

129
00:09:47,200 --> 00:09:50,400
Was they just Apple just spending just paying developers basically?

130
00:09:50,640 --> 00:09:52,480
No, no, no, they set it up with brands.

131
00:09:52,800 --> 00:09:57,120
So like massive brands like Disney and I worked on the dang SDK.

132
00:09:58,800 --> 00:09:59,520
You were inside Apple.

133
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:00,720
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

134
00:10:00,800 --> 00:10:01,680
When iAds was coming out.

135
00:10:01,760 --> 00:10:02,480
Yeah, yeah, I was.

136
00:10:02,960 --> 00:10:04,240
Yeah, it worked and it worked really well.

137
00:10:04,400 --> 00:10:06,400
So we were like, okay, that's, you know, interesting.

138
00:10:07,040 --> 00:10:12,000
We're starting to make some money with that, even though it wasn't like really the plan at the time.

139
00:10:12,240 --> 00:10:15,440
So we kept it free and I think in December 2009.

140
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:17,680
So we introduced a paid version of the app.

141
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:20,720
So there was a free version and there was a paid upfront version.

142
00:10:21,200 --> 00:10:24,560
The next year, I think that in-app purchase was introduced.

143
00:10:24,880 --> 00:10:28,640
So then we had in-app purchases in the, so as a lifetime, you know?

144
00:10:29,200 --> 00:10:31,200
That was basically to remove the ads, yeah.

145
00:10:31,600 --> 00:10:36,000
And that was to remove the ads and slowly we introduced like advanced features.

146
00:10:36,400 --> 00:10:40,240
If you want to export to Dropbox quickly, so this was called Genius Can Plus.

147
00:10:40,640 --> 00:10:41,840
That was a premium plan.

148
00:10:42,480 --> 00:10:48,720
That would have been fairly early, like back in, I mean, I guess if you were doing it not as a subscription but as a, as a one-time in-app purchase.

149
00:10:49,200 --> 00:10:57,760
I think it was as soon as, I mean, we basically Apple announced it and then we announced in that purchase the availability and we, you know, we benefited from that.

150
00:10:58,080 --> 00:11:02,960
Yeah, I wish I could go back in time and and do a better freemium strategy for some of my old apps.

151
00:11:03,360 --> 00:11:09,920
I mean, people don't even realize how good we have it these days with subscriptions and everything else because my apps, I had a free version.

152
00:11:10,560 --> 00:11:16,240
So my like one of my early apps was Gas Cubby tracks fuel economy and vehicle maintenance.

153
00:11:16,480 --> 00:11:17,920
It was actually quite successful.

154
00:11:18,080 --> 00:11:24,800
I sold it and did really well from it, but I had Gas Cubby Lite that was a free version that had limited features.

155
00:11:25,920 --> 00:11:31,120
And then when you reached the limit, you got pushed to the paid upfront app.

156
00:11:31,920 --> 00:11:40,320
And so, I mean, man, back then it was just such, like there were no kind of free trials and like the multiple SKUs and, uh, early days of the app store.

157
00:11:40,640 --> 00:11:44,400
It's crazy how much better things have actually gotten on the monetization front.

158
00:11:44,640 --> 00:11:51,760
But, I mean, happy accident, honestly, because if you had been paid upfront, we wouldn't be here today.

159
00:11:51,920 --> 00:11:52,400
I think, yeah.

160
00:11:52,800 --> 00:12:00,800
What was the story for you and your cofounder from it becoming this like side project, sort of like curiosity to like, hey, this is, this is something worth working on.

161
00:12:01,280 --> 00:12:04,960
Like when did you make enough money that you could like not have to worry about a job?

162
00:12:05,440 --> 00:12:06,800
We didn't want to be entrepreneurs.

163
00:12:06,960 --> 00:12:09,840
Like, I mean, originally it's like we no, but we wanted to make an app.

164
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:12,960
And it's where we're not predestined to be entrepreneurs, basically.

165
00:12:13,120 --> 00:12:22,560
So when we did this app in 2010 and then we graduated in the summer, so Guillaume, my cofounder, went back to France and he started working for a company in France.

166
00:12:22,720 --> 00:12:23,680
And I stayed in the US.

167
00:12:24,080 --> 00:12:24,880
I went to Seattle.

168
00:12:24,960 --> 00:12:25,840
I started working on Amazon.

169
00:12:26,720 --> 00:12:30,000
And then we kept working on it nights and weekends.

170
00:12:30,560 --> 00:12:32,480
And it kind of picked up.

171
00:12:32,880 --> 00:12:40,560
I mean, it kept growing, you know, slowly, but it kept growing with that work., I think we did, I don't exactly remember when, but we started the Android version of the app.

172
00:12:41,200 --> 00:12:46,320
And after a while, then I moved back to San Francisco actually to work for a YC company.

173
00:12:46,480 --> 00:12:50,960
And finally, story also, they contacted me because they found the scanning app and they had scanning needs.

174
00:12:51,040 --> 00:12:57,280
So it was called one thousand memories and they were doing like a scanning of old photos, family photos.

175
00:12:57,600 --> 00:12:59,600
And I kept working on the site, you know, on GeneScan.

176
00:12:59,920 --> 00:13:05,840
So after a while, we were like, okay, we work for our day job, but we also work on that.

177
00:13:06,560 --> 00:13:09,280
We're really like, I mean, it means that we're really like that.

178
00:13:09,680 --> 00:13:10,400
We should go full time.

179
00:13:10,640 --> 00:13:13,680
And that went in 2014, we went full time on it.

180
00:13:13,920 --> 00:13:17,520
We had, you know, enough money to make a living on it.

181
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:20,800
It was already making enough money that you didn't have to, like, starve.

182
00:13:21,120 --> 00:13:23,120
Yeah, that's I mean, we can talk about that later.

183
00:13:23,280 --> 00:13:25,600
But basically, we didn't have to raise funds, right?

184
00:13:25,760 --> 00:13:26,800
We just did it, yeah.

185
00:13:26,960 --> 00:13:35,360
I think also, did you, did you find that once you had made the jump and like once this was your main thing, did you find it, I mean, obviously, you have more time to work on it.

186
00:13:35,840 --> 00:13:43,040
But I also feel like if it's your only source of income, you're a little more, like, aggressive and motivated to, like, make it work better and, like, grow it and things like that.

187
00:13:43,200 --> 00:13:43,920
If it's all you have.

188
00:13:44,400 --> 00:13:47,680
So I think when we started, we had, like, some, I mean, we could live out of it.

189
00:13:47,840 --> 00:13:50,400
So we were not, like grinding to make a living.

190
00:13:50,880 --> 00:13:56,880
So I think if we were like if it was just enough to live, maybe we would what you describe is true in our case.

191
00:13:57,200 --> 00:13:59,680
Yes, I mean, we were full time on it.

192
00:13:59,840 --> 00:14:07,280
We had the time to maybe explore something that we couldn't have otherwise and then grow a team, something we didn't want at the beginning, but over time.

193
00:14:07,840 --> 00:14:15,760
You know, for those listening and kind of maybe tuning out at this point, thinking, ah, I don't have a time machine to get back to 2009 and build a scanning app.

194
00:14:16,160 --> 00:14:21,520
I actually think today we're kind of seeing this new wave with like AI, AR is coming.

195
00:14:22,160 --> 00:14:23,360
Like what was unique?

196
00:14:23,680 --> 00:14:32,560
What's so interesting about this story is that there was a combination of multiple technologies that came along that you leveraged into a business and it grew over time.

197
00:14:32,960 --> 00:14:37,840
So today there's all sorts of new stuff coming out with AI, with new sensors, with new.

198
00:14:38,240 --> 00:14:48,080
And so like there's been so many different points along the history of apps that new things have come out that create new opportunities to create new businesses.

199
00:14:48,320 --> 00:14:51,120
And sometimes it does take years and years and years to grow.

200
00:14:52,080 --> 00:15:02,960
Indy's out there listening when it's like three years in and you haven't made that much progress, but then the fourth year, it's like, wow, I can actually go full time, like it happens and I think it can still happen today.

201
00:15:03,280 --> 00:15:03,360
Yeah.

202
00:15:03,600 --> 00:15:06,720
And even at the time, uh, everyone was saying the gold rush was over.

